Previously, `doNotInstrumentTopLevelModCtor` will skip all module constructor, even if they contain a inner class. This behaviour is now fixed. The instrumentation should only be disabled for actual code inside the constructor and not inner class or functions, as those functions and class could potentially be used later.

From the config description, this is mainly used for resolving a cyclic dependency problem while preserving stack safety checks.
```scala
// Skips instrumenting module constructors, this can be used when the file is statically known to not
// raise any effect and cannot use the Runtime.mls module during module construction due to cyclic dependency.
// One specific scenario is Rendering.mls, which Runtime.mls depends on, and hence using stack safety will
// reference Runtime.mls during construction of the Rendering module, causing a cyclic dependency error.
doNotInstrumentTopLevelModCtor: Bool = false,
```

This PR adds a `CaseOfCase` (COC) optimization pass to `BlockSimplifier`. It simplifies a match whose scrutinee was produced by an earlier match assigning known constructor/literal shapes, allowing nested matches such as chained `Option.map` calls to fuse into a single producer match when doing so is safe.

The implementation also:

- runs DCE to a local fixed point before later optimization passes, so unused inliner labels do not block COC;
- factors constructor-shape detection for calls vs instantiations;
- keeps duplication controlled by the existing inlining threshold;
- refreshes copied consumer branches to preserve symbol hygiene;
- adds regression coverage for full fusion, partial-known producers, prefix dependency safety, large-branch duplication limits, and infinite inlining behavior.

Most other changes are updated golden outputs from the new optimization behavior.

The lifter currently generates useless functions and `isMut` parameters. For example, in:
```fs
:lift
:sir
fun f(x) =
  class C with
    fun y = x
  new C
//│ ———————————————| Lowered IR |———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
//│ let C⁰, f⁰, C$⁰;
//│ define C$⁰ as fun C$¹(isMut, x) {
//│   let tmp;
//│   match isMut
//│     true =>
//│       set tmp = new mut C¹(x);
//│       return tmp
//│     else
//│       set tmp = new C¹(x);
//│       return tmp
//│   end
//│ };
//│ define C⁰ as class C¹ {
//│   private val x⁰;
//│   constructor(x) {
//│     set x⁰ = x;
//│     end
//│   }
//│   method y⁰ = fun y¹ { return x⁰ }
//│ };
//│ define f⁰ as fun f¹(x) { return C$¹(false, x) };
//│ end
//│ —————————————————| Output |—————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
```
We should not be generating the `C$¹` function: we should be using `return new C¹(x)` instead of `return C$¹(false, x1)`.

The only case when we want to generate such a function is if the original class has a parameter list (so it can be used as a function), as in `class C(x)`, and if it is indeed used as a function, as in `C(42)` or `xs.map(C)`. Moreover, the constructor function of parameterized classes always constructs immutable instances, so we should never add an `isMut` parameter to these generated functions.
